# Project Rules — flyrank-frontend

## Context
This repository is the capstone project for the FlyRank internship,
Frontend AI Engineering track. It is currently in the setup phase;
the specific application to be built will be defined in a later assignment.

## Stack (to be finalized)
- Language: JavaScript/TypeScript (preferred once a framework is chosen)
- Frontend framework: TBD (likely React, given the track focus)
- Package manager: npm
- Node.js: LTS version

## Conventions
- Commit messages follow Conventional Commits (e.g. `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:`)
- Keep components small and single-purpose once frontend work begins
- Prefer clear, descriptive naming over abbreviations
- Document any AI-assisted code generation in commit messages where relevant

## AI assistant guidance
- When suggesting code, favor readable, well-commented solutions over clever one-liners,
  since this is a learning/internship context
- Flag any security or environment-variable handling concerns explicitly
- Ask before introducing new dependencies


- Precise, verifiable prompts (explicit constraints + a test/verification step) beat vague prompts — vague prompts shift review burden onto me instead of the AI.
- Branch each round of an AI experiment from a truly clean base (main, before any scaffolding exists) — branching after one round already built an app makes diffs noisy and hard to compare.
- Don't assume accessibility (aria-invalid, aria-describedby, proper labeling) is included unless explicitly requested in the prompt — AI tools don't reliably add it by default.